      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope – Joint Control" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Three Categories of Joint Arrangement" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Jointly Controlled Operations" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Jointly Controlled Assets" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Jointly Controlled Enterprises – THE Genuine Choice" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Why ASPE Retains Proportionate Consolidation" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example – Proportionate Consolidation of a Jointly Controlled Enterprise" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example – the Equity Method for the Same Facts" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to IFRS 11" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" }
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">





<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Governing the accounting for jointly controlled arrangements, with genuine flexibility</div><div class="principle">ASPE Section 3056 governs the ACCOUNTING for INTERESTS in ARRANGEMENTS subject TO joint CONTROL – RETAINING an OLDER, THREE-category STRUCTURE and a GENUINE choice OF accounting METHODS FOR jointly CONTROLLED enterprises, RATHER than ADOPTING IFRS 11'S newer, TWO-category structure and MANDATORY equity METHOD (Section 7).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope – Joint Control</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Contractually agreed sharing of control</div><div class="principle">JOINT control OF an ECONOMIC activity is the CONTRACTUALLY agreed SHARING of the CONTINUING power TO determine ITS strategic OPERATING, investing, AND financing POLICIES – REQUIRING the UNANIMOUS consent OF the parties SHARING control FOR strategic DECISIONS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Three Categories of Joint Arrangement</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A structure that predates, and differs from, IFRS 11's two-category split</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Category</span><span>Description</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Jointly controlled operations</span><span>EACH venturer USES its OWN assets and INCURS its OWN expenses, WITH the ARRANGEMENT lacking a SEPARATE legal STRUCTURE</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Jointly controlled assets</span><span>VENTURERS jointly CONTROL, and OFTEN jointly OWN, specific ASSETS dedicated TO the joint VENTURE'S purposes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1fr 2fr;"><span>Jointly controlled enterprises</span><span>A SEPARATE legal ENTITY (e.g. a CORPORATION or PARTNERSHIP) in WHICH each VENTURER holds an INTEREST</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Jointly Controlled Operations</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Recognizing your own direct share, similar to IFRS 11's joint operations</div><div class="text-supporting">EACH venturer RECOGNIZES ITS OWN assets, LIABILITIES, and EXPENSES incurred, AND its SHARE of INCOME earned, FROM the JOINTLY controlled OPERATION – a TREATMENT BROADLY analogous TO how a JOINT operation is ACCOUNTED for UNDER IFRS 11.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Jointly Controlled Assets</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A proportionate share of the jointly held assets</div><div class="text-supporting">EACH venturer RECOGNIZES ITS SHARE of the JOINTLY controlled ASSETS, ANY liabilities it HAS incurred, ITS share OF liabilities incurred JOINTLY, and ITS share OF income and EXPENSES arising FROM the arrangement.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Jointly Controlled Enterprises – THE Genuine Choice</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Proportionate consolidation, cost method, or equity method</div><div class="text-supporting">FOR a JOINTLY controlled ENTERPRISE (a SEPARATE legal ENTITY), a VENTURER elects EITHER PROPORTIONATE consolidation (COMBINING its OWN proportionate SHARE of the ENTERPRISE'S assets, LIABILITIES, income, AND expenses LINE-by-line WITHIN its OWN financial STATEMENTS), the COST method, or the EQUITY method – a GENUINE THREE-way choice, PARALLELING the SAME optionality WE saw under SECTION 1591'S subsidiary ACCOUNTING (see the COMPANION poster).</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Why ASPE Retains Proportionate Consolidation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A deliberate departure from IFRS 11's 2011 reform</div><div class="text-supporting">BEFORE 2011, IAS 31 (IFRS'S OWN predecessor STANDARD) permitted PROPORTIONATE consolidation AS an ALTERNATIVE for JOINT ventures. IFRS 11 ELIMINATED this OPTION internationally, REQUIRING the EQUITY method EXCLUSIVELY for JOINT ventures. ASPE, BY contrast, RETAINED the OLDER, more FLEXIBLE approach – CONSISTENT with ITS broader PHILOSOPHY of GIVING private ENTERPRISES genuine METHOD choices FOR group-level RELATIONSHIPS (SUBSIDIARIES, significant-INFLUENCE investments, and NOW jointly controlled ENTERPRISES ALIKE), RATHER than IMPOSING a SINGLE mandatory APPROACH the WAY current IFRS DOES.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example – Proportionate Consolidation of a Jointly Controlled Enterprise</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Combining the venturer's own proportionate share, line by line</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A 50/50 joint venture entity</div><div class="wk-body">Two companies jointly control a separate corporation, each holding 50%. The jointly controlled enterprise has total assets of $6,000,000, total liabilities of $2,400,000, and revenue of $3,200,000 for the year.</div><div class="wk-answer">EACH venturer, ELECTING proportionate CONSOLIDATION, includes 50% of EACH line ITEM within ITS OWN financial STATEMENTS: ASSETS of $3,000,000, LIABILITIES of $1,200,000, and REVENUE of <strong>$1,600,000</strong> – COMBINED line-by-LINE with the VENTURER'S own OTHER assets, LIABILITIES, and REVENUE.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example – the Equity Method for the Same Facts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A single investment line instead of line-by-line combination</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: Choosing the equity method instead</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, the joint venture entity's net income for the year is $500,000. One of the venturers instead elects the equity method.</div><div class="wk-answer">The VENTURER recognizes a SINGLE "INVESTMENT in JOINT venture" LINE, ADJUSTED for ITS 50% share OF net INCOME ($500,000 × 50% = <strong>$250,000</strong>) – RATHER than COMBINING the ENTERPRISE'S individual ASSETS, LIABILITIES, and REVENUE line-BY-line, as PROPORTIONATE consolidation WOULD require.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What Section 3056 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature of the joint arrangement, and the accounting method elected</span><span>Aggregate amount of the venturer's share of assets, liabilities, income, and expenses of jointly controlled arrangements</span><span>Nature and extent of any contingent liabilities arising from joint arrangements</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgment matters most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether joint control genuinely exists</span><span>Which of the three method alternatives best serves the venturer's users, for a jointly controlled enterprise</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Generally minimal beyond those inherent in the chosen method's own mechanics</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to IFRS 11</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison – ASPE Section 3056 vs IFRS 11</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASPE Section 3056</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS 11</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Structure</span><span>Three categories</span><span>Two categories (joint operation / joint venture)</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Proportionate consolidation option</span><span>Available for jointly controlled enterprises</span><span>ELIMINATED in 2011 – equity method mandatory for joint ventures</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">A GENUINE, meaningful STRUCTURAL divergence – ASPE'S retained FLEXIBILITY here MIRRORS the OLDER, pre-2011 IAS 31 APPROACH, RATHER than CURRENT IFRS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every joint arrangement event lands</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Proportionate consolidation</span><span>ALL primary statements</span><span>Line-by-line combination of the venturer's proportionate share</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Equity method share of income</span><span><span class="badge bs">BS</span> <span class="badge is">IS</span></span><span>Single investment line, adjusted for the venturer's share of profit or loss</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 1591</span><span>Subsidiaries – governs a parallel three-way method choice for control-level relationships</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Section 3051</span><span>Investments – supplies the equity method mechanics this Section applies where elected</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Proportionate consolidation</div><div class="glossary-def">Combining a venturer's own proportionate share of a jointly controlled enterprise's assets, liabilities, income, and expenses line-by-line within its own financial statements.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Jointly controlled enterprise</div><div class="glossary-def">A separate legal entity in which two or more venturers hold an interest, subject to joint control.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="legend"><div class="ltitle">Financial statement key (ASPE terminology)</div><div class="lrow"><div class="litem"><span class="badge bs">BS</span> Balance Sheet </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge is">IS</span> Income Statement </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge sre">SRE</span> Statement of Retained Earnings / Statement of Changes in Equity </div><div class="litem"><span class="badge scf">SCF</span> Cash Flow Statement</div></div></div>


</div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.aspe3056 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
